(comment EnglishMt "The mt for semantic mappings between Cyc terms and words or phrases in english which are not \"general\".  For general phrases and words, make the assertion in the #$GeneralEnglishMt (a #$genlMt of this one).  See the #$NoteOnCriteriaForGeneralEnglishMt for more on what constitues a general english lexical assertion.")
(comment ScrubbingCleanser "Powdered scrubbing cleanser, such as Comet and Ajax.")
(comment FrameForAdverbs "The class of #$SubcategorizationFrames which make sense for #$Adverbs.")
(comment FrameForAdjectives "The class of #$SubcategorizationFrames which make sense for #$Adjectives.")
(comment DefiniteDeterminerFrame "This frame applies to nouns which typically occur with the definite determiner, for example, 'the Sun'; 'the flu'.")
(comment YupikLanguage "An Eskimo language spoken mainly in Alaska and Siberia.")
(comment AttributiveOnlyAdjective "The collection of all adjectives which can *only* be used attributively. For example, 'former' can only be used that way as in 'the former president', but not predicatively as in 'the president is former'. Compare with #$PredicativeOnlyAdjective and #$AttributivePredicativeAdjective.")
(comment NonIntersectiveAdjective "The collection of all non-intersective adjectives. The semantics of a non-intersective adjective-noun combination does not imply the semantics of the noun alone. For example, if someone is an 'alleged burglar', this does not imply that s/he is a burglar. By contrast, such an inference is true for #$IntersectiveAdjectives like 'red' (refer to the comment on #$IntersectiveAdjective).")
(comment PredicativeOnlyAdjective "The collection of all adjectives which can *only* be used predicatively. Compare with #$AttributiveOnlyAdjective and #$AttributivePredicativeAdjective.")
(comment adjSemTrans-Restricted "(#$adjSemTrans-Restricted WU SENSE FRAME COL TRANS) means that an adjective form of WU, when modifying a noun that denotes a spec of COL, with sense number SENSE and frame FRAME, means TRANS. For instance, (#$adjSemTrans-Restricted #$Sterile-TheWord 0 #$RegularAdjFrame #$Artifact (#$dirtinessOfObject :NOUN #$Sterile)) means that a phrase like 'sterile knife' can be interpreted as meaning (#$and (#$isa ?NOUN #$Knife) (#$dirtinessOfObject ?NOUN #$Sterile)), whereas for 'sterile man', this interpretation is not available.")
(comment CC-PennTag "The tag used for #$CoordinatingConjunctions like 'and' or 'or' and double conjunctions like 'either', 'neither' and 'both'.")
(comment CD-PennTag "The tag used for cardinal numbers used as #$Adjectives like '4' and 'seventeen'.")
(comment COM-PennTag "The tag used for the #$PunctuationSymbol #$Comma-TheSymbol ','. The actual tag used is ',', which is not a valid #$Cyc constant name.")
(comment DBQ-PennTag "The tag used for #$QuotationMark-Double. The actual tag used is the double quote, which is not a valid #$Cyc constant name.")
(comment DOL-PennTag "The tag used for the #$DollarSign-TheSymbol '$'. The actual tag used is '$', which is not a valid #$Cyc constant name.")
(comment DT-PennTag "The tag used for determiners,  which do not fall under any of the categories PDT, JJ, PRP$, PRP, WDT or WP$ like 'this', 'each' and 'another', pronoun #$Determiners like 'any' and 'some', pronoun plural #$Determiners like 'these' and 'those', pronoun or double conjunction #$Determiners like 'both', 'either' and 'neither, and articles like 'the', 'a' and 'an'.")
(comment EX-PennTag "The tag used for the #$There-Existential 'there'.")
(comment FW-PennTag "The tag used for foreign words not in the English lexicon.")
(comment JJ-PennTag "The tag used for #$Adjectives in their #$regularDegree like 'happy' and 'bad', for ordinal numbers like 'second' and 'last', #$Adjectives in their semantic superlative like 'chief' and 'top', and postdeterminers like 'many' and 'same'.")
(comment JJR-PennTag "The tag used for #$Adjectives in their #$comparativeDegree like 'happier' and 'worse'.")
(comment JJS-PennTag "The tag used for #$Adjectives in their #$superlativeDegree like 'happiest' and 'worst'.")
(comment LS-PennTag "The tag used for list items like 'A' and 'First'.")
(comment MD-PennTag "The tag used for #$Modal verbs like 'can', could, and ''ll'.")
(comment NNPS-PennTag "The tag used for #$ProperNouns in their #$plural from like 'Australians' or 'Methodists'.")
(comment OPP-PennTag "The tag used for any kind of opening parentheses like OpenParen-TheSymbol, OpenSquareBracket-TheSymbol and OpenCurlyBracket-TheSymbol. The actual tag used is '(', which is not a valid Cyc constant name.")
(comment PDT-PennTag "The tag used for prequalifier and prequantifier #$Determiners like 'quite', 'all' and 'half'.")
(comment PER-PennTag "The tag used for the #$PunctuationSymbols Period-TheSymbol '.', ExclamationPoint-TheSymbol '!' and #$QuestionMark-TheSymbol '?'. The actual tag used is '.', which is not a valid Cyc constant name.")
(comment POS-PennTag "The tag used for the #$PossessiveMarkers 's and '.")
(comment PRP-PennTag "The tag used for possessive #$Determiners like 'mine' and 'yours', for #$SubjectPronouns and #$ObjectPronouns like 'you', 'she', and 'them',  and for #$ReflexivePronouns like 'herself' and 'ourselves' .")
(comment RB-PennTag "The tag used for unmarked #$Adverbs which do not fall under any of the  #$PennPosTags RBR, RBS or WRB like 'often' and 'particularly', negative #$Adverbs like 'not' and 'n't', degree #$Adverbs like 'very', 'so' and 'too', postposed degree #$Adverbs like 'enough' and 'indeed', and nominal #$Adverbs like 'here', 'there' and 'now'.")
(comment RBR-PennTag "The tag used for #$Adverbs in their #$comparativeDegree like 'slower'.")
(comment RBS-PennTag "The tag used for #$Adverbs in their #$superlativeDegree like 'slowest'.")
(comment RP-PennTag "The tag used for particle #$Adverbs like 'up', 'off' and 'out'.")
(comment SYM-PennTag "The tag used for symbols like '[fj]', '<' and '*'.")
(comment TO-PennTag "The tag used for the #$InfinitiveComp 'to' and the #$Preposition 'to'.")
(comment UH-PennTag "The tag used for #$Interjection-SpeechParts and other isolated material like 'oh', 'yes' and 'mmmmm'.")
(comment VB-PennTag "The tag used for the infinite form of #$MainVerbs and nonmodal #$AuxVerbs like 'take', 'live', 'do', 'have' and 'be'.")
(comment VBD-PennTag "The tag used for #$MainVerbs and nonmodal #$AuxVerbs in the past tense like 'took', 'lived', 'did', 'had' and 'was'.")
(comment VBG-PennTag "The tag used for #$MainVerbs and nonmodal #$AuxVerbs in the present participle form like 'taking', 'living', 'doing', 'having' and 'being'.")
(comment VBN-PennTag "The tag used for #$MainVerbs and nonmodal #$AuxVerbs in the past/passive participle form like 'taken', 'lived', 'done', 'had' and 'been'.")
(comment VBP-PennTag "The tag used for present tense, non third singular form of #$MainVerbs and nonmodal #$AuxVerbs like 'take', 'live', 'do', 'have' and 'am'.")
(comment VBZ-PennTag "The tag used for #$MainVerbs and nonmodal #$AuxVerb in the present, 3rd singular form like 'takes', 'lives', 'does', 'has', 'is', and ''s'.")
(comment WDT-PennTag "The tag used for question #$Determiners like 'which' and 'whatever'.")
(comment WP-PennTag "The tag used for possessive and qustion #$Determiners lik e'whose'.")
(comment WP-PennTag "The tag used for question #$Pronouns like 'who' and 'whoever'.")
(comment WRB-PennTag "The tag used for question or degree and question #$Adverbs like 'when', 'how', 'why' or 'how' and 'however'.")
(comment IN-PennTag "The tag used for #$SubordinatingConjunctions like 'although' and 'when', for the #$Complementizer 'that', and for all #$Prepositions other than 'to' like 'for', 'above' and 'of'.")
(comment PRPD-PennTag "The tag used for possessive #$Determiners like 'their' and 'your'. The actual tag used is PRP$, which is not a valid #$Cyc constant name")
(comment WPD-PennTag "The tag used for possessive and qustion #$Determiners lik e'whose'. The actual tag used is WP$, which is not a valid #$Cyc constant name")
(comment POU-PennTag "The tag used for the pound sign. The actual tag used is '#', which is not a valid #$Cyc constant name.")
(comment LDQ-PennTag "The tag used for the left double quote ``. The actual tag used is ``, which is not a valid #$Cyc constant name.")
(comment LSQ-PennTag "The tag used for the left single quote `. The actual tag used is `, which is not a valid #$Cyc constant name.")
(comment RDQ-PennTag "The tag used for the right double quote ''. The actual tag used is '', which is not a valid #$Cyc constant name.")
(comment RSQ-PennTag "The tag used for the right single quote '. The actual tag used is ', which is not a valid #$Cyc constant name.")
(comment ClausalSubjectTransitiveNPFrame "This frame is used for transitive words (usually verbs or adjectives) requiring a complement-clausal subject and exactly one complement which is an NP. All of these can also be used with an 'it' subject and a that-clause object. Example: 'That John didn't go to the party disappointed Mary', ('It disappointed Mary that John didn't go to the party'), 'That Fred took over the project was good for Mary', ('It was good for Mary that Fred took over the project'). Contrast with #$PleonasticThatClauseFrame.")
(comment TransitiveQuoteFrame "This frame is used for transitive words that take exactly one complement that appears in quotes.  Example: the verb \"say\" in \"Sally said 'Don't go!'\".")
(comment DitransitiveNP-QuoteFrame "This frame is used for transitive words that take exactly two complements such that the final one appears in quotes.  Example: the verb *entitle* in \"Fred entitled the book \"Days of yore\".")
(comment StatueOfPaulRevere-Boston "The statue of Paul Revere on horseback in the Paul Revere Mall of Boston's James Rego Square.")
(comment semTransPredForPOS "(#$semTransPredForPOS POS PRED) means that PRED is the #$SemTransPred corresponding to the Cyc #$SpeechPart POS.")
(comment MAYAForthWorthConference "the Muslim American Youth Association conference in Ft. Worth, Texas, during 1983, at which Sheik Abdallah Azzam was a guest speaker")
(comment dontAddTheToNamesWithPred "(#$dontAddTheToNamesWithPred NAME-STRING-PRED) means that names asserted using PRED are exempt from the policy of adding 'the' to the beginning of names that look like they might need it.")
(comment dontAddTheToNamesOfInstances "(#$dontAddTheToNamesOfInstances COLLECTION) means that names asserted on instances of COLLECTION are exempt from the policy of adding 'the' to the beginning of names that look like they might need it.")
(comment syntacticCategoryTags "(#$syntacticCategoryTags CAT TAG) means that CAT is the most specific syntactic category that a word or phrase tagged with TAG can be assumed to have by virtue of that tagging.")
(comment PennPosTag "The collection of part of speech tags developed for the #$EnglishLanguage by the UniversityOfPennsylvania. Each part of speech tag is roughly akin to conventional parts of speech, but more detailed in that it incorporates morphological distinctions like different degrees of #$Adjectives, #$singular and #$plural form of #$Nouns, and different inflections of #$Verbs. A scheme different from #$PennPosTags is used to organize the lexicon in #$Cyc, but since many tagged corpora use  #$PennPosTags, the quantitive information contained in those corpora can be used for more efficient parsing.")
(comment pennTagString "(#$pennTagString TAG STRING) means that TAG is typically represented in non-CycL parser output as STRING.")
(comment NN-PennTag "The tag used for #$MassNouns like 'milk', for #$CountNouns and #$AgentiveNouns used in the #$singular form like 'woman', for adverbial nouns like 'tomorrow' and 'home' and for nominal indefinite #$Pronouns like 'none', 'everything' and 'one'.")
(comment CLP-PennTag "The tag used for any kind of closing parentheses like #$CloseParen-TheSymbol, #$CloseSquareBracket-TheSymbol and #$CloseCurlyBracket-TheSymbol.")
(comment COL-PennTag "The tag used for the #$PunctuationSymbols #$Semicolon-TheSymbol ';', #$Colon-TheSymbol ':', and #$Ellipsis-TheSymbol '...'.")
(comment NNS-PennTag "The tag used for #$CountNouns and #$AgentiveNouns in their #$plural form like 'women' or 'runners', and for adverbial plural nouns like 'Sundays' and 'weekdays'.")
(comment agreementPredicateTags "<code>(#$agreementPredicateTags PRED TAG)</code> means that <code>PRED</code> is the most specific agreement predicate that a word or phrase tagged with <code>TAG</code> can be assumed to have by virtue of that tagging.")
(comment NNP-PennTag "The tag used for #$ProperNouns and names in their #$nonPlural-Generic or #$nameString form like 'John' and 'London'.")
(comment Emolient "The collection of substances that soften and moisturize the skin and decrease itchiness and flaking.")
(comment WaikatoDigitalLibrariesLab "The Digital Libraries Lab at the University of Waikato.  Members who are interested in expanding Cyc include #$CathyLegg and Olena Medyelan.")
(comment Carabiner "The collection of carabiners, small metallic loops with a gate, used to secure ropes during instances of #$RockClimbing.")
(genls PennPosTag NonlexicalLinguisticObject)
(genlPreds futureTense-Generic firstPersonSg-Generic)
(genlPreds futureTense-Generic secondPersonSg-Generic)
(genlPreds futureTense-Generic thirdPersonSg-Generic)
(genlPreds futureTense-Generic pluralVerb-Generic)
(genls AttributiveOnlyAdjective Adjective)
(genls NonIntersectiveAdjective Adjective)
